  Release Notes for 0.1.0

  (Written 2026-09-17 from the `pdf-organizer` repository, which holds
  the project's first three weeks — 26 April to 17 May 2026. 0.1.0 was
  prepared at the time: "pre-v0.1.0: lock on-disk format", "README and
  Makefile polish for v0.1.0", three Sharing items explicitly deferred
  from it. It was then never tagged and never written up, and the
  version in pyproject.toml was relabelled down to 0.0.1. These notes
  record what the first release was.)

 o FEATURE: The premise, which nothing since has changed: a plain
            directory of PDFs. Each paper gets a sidecar JSON file
            beside it and a thumbnail; an SQLite index is built from
            the sidecars for searching. Drop a file into the folder
            and a watcher imports it. Nothing is uploaded anywhere

 o FEATURE: Metadata without typing: title, authors, year, DOI and
            journal from the PDF's own XMP, from CrossRef, from
            OpenAlex, or from the text of page 1 — and from the
            filename where a publisher encodes the DOI in it

 o FEATURE: A built-in PDF viewer, with text selection delegated to
            Poppler, highlights in the margin and comments attached
            to them. Both are stored in the sidecar, so they travel
            with the paper rather than living in a database

 o FEATURE: Clickable citations in the body text. Click [12] and get
            the resolved reference with its metadata, and the option
            to add it to the library. Numbered bibliographies first,
            then author-year ones (Acta Cryst and other IUCr titles
            were the working case) resolved against OpenAlex

 o FEATURE: A References panel per paper, with related-works and
            cited-by popovers, from OpenAlex and falling back to
            CrossRef's reference list where OpenAlex has none

 o FEATURE: BibTeX import and export, including "ghost" entries: an
            imported .bib makes a card for every entry, PDF or not.
            "Get PDF" then fills a ghost in — open-access sources
            first, Unpaywall next, and the publisher's landing-page
            citation_pdf_url when Unpaywall lags behind

 o FEATURE: Citations in the styles people are actually asked for:
            "Cite as…" through CSL, plus RIS and CSL JSON export

 o FEATURE: An author view: what someone has published recently, and
            a citing-impact score split by whether the citing paper
            treats the work as software, as a method, or as an idea.
            That split is the unusual one — "cited as software" is a
            different claim from "cited as an idea"

 o FEATURE: Discover: search OpenAlex by author or topic without
            leaving the application

 o FEATURE: Subscriptions. Follow a journal or a saved search and
            collect what is new into a feed, refreshed in the
            background, with papers you already hold badged as such

 o FEATURE: Full-text search over the library — including your own
            highlight and comment text, so a paper can be found by
            what you said about it. JSON columns are stored as raw
            Unicode so that names with diacritics are findable

 o FEATURE: Sort order is remembered between sessions

 o CHANGE:  Care taken for shared and networked home directories:
            the database filename carries the hostname so two
            machines sharing an NFS $HOME cannot collide, sidecar
            temporary files are hostname-suffixed, the database is
            WAL-checkpointed and closed on shutdown, and a warning
            appears when the SQLite cache is sitting on a network
            filesystem where it does not belong

 o CHANGE:  OpenAlex is treated as a shared resource: a session
            circuit-breaker on repeated rate-limit responses, a
            credit budget for background refreshers, and caching so
            that browsing does not spend a daily allowance

 o CHANGE:  The on-disk format was deliberately frozen before
            release — sidecar JSON beside the PDF, thumbnail beside
            it, index derived and disposable — so that later versions
            could change their minds about everything else

  (At this point the Python package was still named `pdforg`, and the
  application was renamed to Alexandria on its second day.)
